Reproducers for the batched RI fast-path FK check
=================================================

This directory holds reproducers for bugs found while reviewing the batched
fast-path foreign-key check added by

    b7b27eb41a5  Optimize fast-path FK checks with batched index probes

and its follow-ups (5c54c3ed1b9, 0e47bb5fbee, 4113873a5ab, plus the
"cross-type", "per firing cycle" and "per subtransaction" fixes).

The scripts live in `repro/` rather than `t/`, and this directory is not
listed in `src/test/modules/Makefile` or `meson.build`, because every
reproducer either crashes the backend or asserts on behaviour that is
currently wrong.  Run one explicitly with:

    make -C src/test/modules/ri_fastpath_repro check \
        PROVE_TESTS=repro/001_setconstraints_subxact.pl

(`configure --enable-tap-tests` is required; `--enable-cassert` changes the
symptom of issues 1 and 3 from a data-structure corruption into an assertion
failure, and both variants are accepted by the scripts.)

Each test is written so that it *passes* once the corresponding bug is fixed.


Background
----------

`RI_FKey_check()` no longer probes the PK index per row.  When after-trigger
firing is active it buffers the FK row in an `RI_FastPathEntry`, keyed by
`{constraint oid, after-trigger query depth}`, and registers
`ri_FastPathEndBatch()` through `RegisterAfterTriggerBatchCallback()`.  The
batch is probed when it reaches `RI_FASTPATH_BATCH_SIZE` (64) rows, or when
the callback fires at the end of the firing cycle.

`RegisterAfterTriggerBatchCallback()` puts the callback on
`afterTriggers.query_stack[query_depth].batch_callbacks` when
`query_depth >= 0`, and on the transaction-wide
`afterTriggers.batch_callbacks` otherwise.  Those two lists are fired by
different callers:

    AfterTriggerEndQuery()      fires query_stack[query_depth].batch_callbacks
    AfterTriggerFireDeferred()  fires afterTriggers.batch_callbacks
    AfterTriggerSetState()      fires afterTriggers.batch_callbacks

The mismatch in the last line is the root of issue 1 and issue 3.


001_setconstraints_subxact.pl -- SET CONSTRAINTS does not flush the batch
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

`SET CONSTRAINTS ALL IMMEDIATE` fires the pending deferred triggers and then
fires only the *transaction-wide* batch-callback list.  But
`AfterTriggerSetState()` can perfectly well run while an after-trigger query
level is open -- `AfterTriggerBeginQuery()` is called for every
INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/MERGE (execMain.c) and for COPY FROM -- and then
`RegisterAfterTriggerBatchCallback()` has put the RI callback on the
*query-level* list instead.  The batch is therefore still buffered when
`SET CONSTRAINTS` returns.

Part 1 shows the visible consequence: the FK violation is not reported by
`SET CONSTRAINTS ALL IMMEDIATE`, but later, by whatever statement happens to
end that query level.

Part 2 shows the memory-safety consequence.  When the `SET CONSTRAINTS` runs
inside a subtransaction that *commits* (a PL/pgSQL `BEGIN ... EXCEPTION`
block), the entry outlives the subtransaction it was created in, which the
code explicitly assumes cannot happen:

    /* ri_triggers.c, ri_FastPathBatchAdd() */
    Assert(fpentry->subid == GetCurrentSubTransactionId());

    /* ri_triggers.c, AtEOSubXact_RI(), isCommit branch */
    Assert(false);

The subtransaction's ResourceOwner has already released `pk_rel`, `idx_rel`
and the two slots' TupleDescs by then, so the entry is left holding relcache
pointers that nothing owns any more:

    WARNING:  resource was not closed: relation "pk_pkey"
    WARNING:  resource was not closed: relation "pk"
    WARNING:  resource was not closed: TupleDesc 0x... (16427,-1)
    WARNING:  resource was not closed: TupleDesc 0x... (16421,-1)
    ERROR:  relcache reference 0x... is not owned by resource owner Portal

and with `--enable-cassert`:

    TRAP: failed Assert("false"), File: "ri_triggers.c", Line: 4543


002_trigger_order.pl -- FK check deferred past later AFTER triggers
-------------------------------------------------------------------

An immediate FK check used to run inside the RI trigger, i.e. interleaved
with the table's other AFTER ROW triggers in trigger-name order.  Batching
moves the actual probe to the end of the whole firing cycle, so an AFTER ROW
trigger that sorts *after* the `RI_ConstraintTrigger_c_NNN` trigger can now
satisfy the constraint retroactively.

The test inserts a row referencing a nonexistent key while an AFTER ROW
trigger inserts that key into the PK table.  With the batched fast path the
INSERT succeeds; the same schema with a partitioned PK table -- which
`ri_fastpath_is_applicable()` sends down the SPI path -- rejects it, as does
unpatched PostgreSQL.

This is a silent change to when a foreign key is enforced, not just a
different error message.


003_reentrant_flush.pl -- re-entrant batch flush (use-after-free)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

`ri_FastPathEndBatch()` guards against re-entry with the file-static
`ri_fastpath_flushing`, which makes a nested `RI_FKey_check()` take the
per-row path instead of touching the cache that is being iterated.  That
guard only covers re-entry through `RI_FKey_check()`.  It does not cover
re-entry through the callback list itself: `SET CONSTRAINTS` fires
`afterTriggers.batch_callbacks` unconditionally, so user code invoked *by the
flush* can call `ri_FastPathEndBatch()` again, on the very entry that is
mid-flush.

The reproducer uses a user-defined implicit cast function (the flush calls it
from `build_index_scankeys()`/`ri_FastPathFlushArray()`, which is exactly the
re-entrancy the code comments anticipate) that runs
`SET CONSTRAINTS ALL IMMEDIATE`:

    AfterTriggerFireDeferred
      FireAfterTriggerBatchCallbacks
        ri_FastPathEndBatch            <- ri_fastpath_flushing = true
          ri_FastPathBatchFlush        <- fpentry->flushing = true
            ri_FastPathFlushArray
              FunctionCall3Coll        <- user cast function
                ... SET CONSTRAINTS ALL IMMEDIATE
                  AfterTriggerSetState
                    FireAfterTriggerBatchCallbacks
                      ri_FastPathEndBatch   <- re-entered

The inner call re-flushes the same entry and then runs
`ri_FastPathTeardown()`, which deletes `flush_cxt` (the buffered heap tuples
and the search values point into it), drops both slots, closes `pk_rel` and
`idx_rel` and removes the entry from the hash table -- all while the outer
`ri_FastPathFlushArray()` is still using them.  Observed symptoms:

  * cassert build: `TRAP: failed Assert("!ri_fastpath_flushing"), File:
    "ri_triggers.c", Line: 4346`
  * regular build: `client backend was terminated by signal 11: Segmentation
    fault`
  * if the cast function does not stop after the first re-entry, the
    re-entrant flush re-runs the cast function, which recurses until
    `ERROR: too many active hash_seq_search scans, cannot start one on "RI
    fast-path cache"` and then `PANIC: ERRORDATA_STACK_SIZE exceeded`, which
    takes down the whole cluster.

The inner `FireAfterTriggerBatchCallbacks()` also clears
`afterTriggers.firing_batch_callbacks` on the way out, defeating the
`Assert(!afterTriggers.firing_batch_callbacks)` in
`RegisterAfterTriggerBatchCallback()` for the rest of the outer flush.
